 on on other  discretionary kinds of things so when we  talk about welfare what are the way you  know there's things like food stamps  what what are the other types of major  major programs that go into what we  welfare I mean a lot of people think of  welfare is just cash assistance and they  think of you know families getting a  check in the mail but for example food  stamps alone has 45 million people there  is public housing assistance there's  there's Medicaid  there's Obama phone which is actually a  phone system that is provided to poor  people but it's been around since Reagan  there before so it's not really the  wrong phone it's just that people have  heard about it and it's a welfare  program that helps people who otherwise  wouldn't have access to a phone now to a  cell phone and the I mean that they  could use it to apply for jobs or you  know just stay in contact with friends  and family talk a little bit about the  interviews that you did I mean there are  people all over the country to talk to  how did you find them how you know how  how did you locate them but then also  how did you find them mm-hm  well I traveled all over the country for  this book I went from Hawaii to  California in Washington State Oregon  Georgia South Dakota and basically I  wanted to know how it felt to actually  be on welfare you know you can read  about what the rules are how you think  it works but then you go out and  actually talk to people about how it  plays out in their real life and so I  would drive into a town and park it a  either soup kitchen or a public park or  bus stops and just look for people who  look like they'd be willing to talk and  sit down and ask them about their town  or you know tell me about this  neighborhood I'm new here I'm visiting  and I'm here writing a book and it's  about welfare would you be willing to  talk to me and I was really amazed at  how open people were to sharing their  stories and telling me about you know  what it was like to be on food stamps  how their Medicaid card worked what they  did with their cash assistance the two  things that struck me was first off is  how incredibly complex all of these  systems seem to be it's a you know -  yeah I mean there were there are people  to go out and recruit people to be on  welfare and whatnot but the systems  themselves seem very complicated and  then how both kind of ingenious and I  don't even mean that in a negative way  but how kind of smart - people are like  they totally know the systems and if you  could talk a little bit maybe in a  general way of they know when if they  you know if they hit a certain threshold  they're going to lose more benefits and  they would gain from having  job or something like talk a little bit  about their the mindset that they used  to calculate their total benefits  package  well they do they would I mean I have  called it doing the math because they  really would they knew exactly down to  the penny how much they would get based  on you know which programs they were in  and how much income they had coming in  and it was actually frightening for them  sometimes to think about Oh taking that  extra hour of work or you know taking  that raise because then they would lose  their benefits because they knew you  know if I go over this line by even a  dollar I could lose my food stamp  benefit I could lose my housing benefit  and when you're not when you're at those  low level jobs that might come and go it  can be kind of scary to take on the  extra income because then you're losing  quite a big benefit that's pretty  dependable so most the people I met knew  exactly how much they could make and  they knew how much they were getting  every month and they relied on it and  it's small sums of money but it's really  important when it's what you're living  on what would you say is how often do  people are is is there a set percentage  you could say that people on welfare on  some type of assistance go into a gray  market so they're working you know above  board and whatnot but then because they  know that next dollar is going to kill  me on my benefits and then they start  doing stuff off the books oh yeah I  think off the books work is very very  common I don't think I met anybody that  wasn't doing some kind of off-the-books  work and and people trade and barter a  lot I mean they trade babysitting for  car maintenance for a place to sleep for  or they'll sell their food stamps you  know in order to get some cash to go pay  for something so there's a very very  active underground economy the  off-the-books part I find rather  encouraging because it people want to  work when they want to earn money they  just don't want to lose their benefits  and I think one of the worst things  about the welfare system is that in it  induces this psychology of fear of  earning too much the prospect of having  all your benefits cut off or some are a  significant part of your benefits are  cut off makes people look  earning income as risky how did that  come to be a defining kind of  characteristic of the system because the  whole idea of work of welfare reform in  the 90s was that it was supposed to be  work fair it was going to be time  limited it was going to push people to  work which is what everybody wants you  nobody wants to be dependent and yet now  you're saying that you know people don't  want to work or they realize that work  is risky that obviously means that  something is terribly wrong in the  system  well the reforms of 1996 which were real  and to a considerable extent effective  we're in the 8th aid to families with  dependent children which was transformed  into a program as you suggest that was  time limited the required work that  offered some training and job placement  but what has happened is that that  program is now about two or three  percent of the total welfare package  it's still there it still has work  requirements but food stamps are much  much bigger no work requirements they're  the combination of these programs for  people who are eligible for them provide  sufficient income to get by but induce a  sense of entrapment  because of the fear of having them cut  off if they earn too much money or if  the caseworker finds out that they are  horror of Horrors actually earning more  money than they're supposed to let's  talk about housing assistance you guys  write that and I'm quoting here  subsidized housing is a major component  of the u.s. federal welfare system with  the cost of the program doubling between  1990 and 2010 even though the number of  recipients has stayed about the same how  how does housing assistance hurt the  poor well probably the biggest problem  with housing assistance is that people  are in public housing that no longer  qualify for it and the reason for that  is because  the way the system was designed was with  the idea that we want people to have a  stable place to live and then they can  work on finding jobs and getting working  their way out of poverty and so we  didn't have the kind of checking in and  making sure that you're still on  benefits that we have with other  programs so for example in my travels I  met people often who said that they had  no hope of getting into the public  housing in the towns or cities that they  lived in because they were completely  full and in many cases full with people  who no longer actually qualified for the  program but there was nobody telling  them to move out so there was no  follow-up to say okay you you're not  making it and so now you should find  your own place yeah and I met one young  gallon in the Bronx and she told me a  story of her aunt who had been in the  same public housing unit for 30 years  and had moved in and out of it rented it  out to other people had long since  stopped being in poverty but no one had  ever checked on her I mean while this  young gal had two kids and really needed  the program and was told that she was on  a waiting list that was ten years long  some original abuse is there I mean if  you if you qualify for housing and then  live in it for a couple of years and you  don't qualify anymore and you rent it  out to your relatives or to somebody  else and you can work your way out of  poverty just as a landlord renting out a  piece of housing that you're not  supposed to have that happens then again  you're right that's pretty inventive I  mean it's a sign of somebody you know if  they were applying that uh ingenuity to  the actual economy it'd be a good thing  Medicaid is health care for the poor and  it's paid for jointly by the states and  the federal government it's a massive  expense in most states Medicaid is  always either the first or second  biggest thing that they they spend money  on and now if you make a certain salary  you don't qualify for Medicaid and a  host of other programs and then you  quoted in a book woman named Annie a  mother of two in Oregon who said I'm not  going to find a job that pays enough to  make up for the value of my Medicaid  coverage for me and my two kids and this  refrain comes up again and again  recipients realize that they're going to  lose a lot more than a little bit they  would gain from  money is is that a ubiquitous problem in  these means-tested programs and then how  how do you deal with that because you  know you need to have a cut-off right  for people where okay you're not getting  assistance anymore but how do you how do  you change that kind of plateau where  people just drop off the face of the  earth if they make a little bit more  well to begin with it you you have to  deal with the culture of the system the  culture of the system is geared to  maintaining itself the people who work  in the welfare business their jobs  depend on it  they have very little incentive to get  people out of welfare and into jobs they  just they they lose some of their  potential income as a result of that one  of the most telling stories that came  out of this process is a man of about  thirty thirty-five and an angel who  related his visits to his welfare office  says you go in there's nothing about  jobs he said you go in on a bulletin  board  need food stamps need help with Medicaid  he said everything is geared in in the  whole system to more welfare different  welfare they should put up a sign he  said is it twelve jobs available this  week in this communities it never  happens so the culture of the of the  system is anti job Pro more welfare and  the only way to change that it seems to  me I mean part of the way is to make  work pay as an e ITC which which we do  need to address but part of it is to  make work looking for work training for  work the part of every one of these  programs you call the Earned Income Tax  Credit Welfare done almost right  describe it for us and then explain you  know why it's not quite done right well  the idea instant is to make work pay by  topping up the wages of very low income  people and we think that makes sense  and  a fairly complicated way to approach it  because it is done through the income  tax system originally designed to repay  income tax withheld from low-income  people now if you're working and your  income is low enough you're going to get  in effect your wages topped up this is I  think the the right approach we think it  would work much much better if instead  of having to file an income tax return  in March or April every year these funds  were simply made available every pay  period so that people would would be  taking home a bigger check rather than  going through the rather complicated IRS  system sometimes with the help of  agencies that are not entirely ethical  in all cases and it only is the EITC  on't basically only available either to  married couples or people with fewer  children I mean this is the other really  big flaw and both conservatives and  liberals most recently President Obama  himself said this is a big mistake we  ought to make a ITC available much more  generously for the childless Phil is a  good libertarian you know like social  engineering through the tax code for  sure so yeah why should people with with  kids get extra money but why should if  you're an able-bodied single person why  should you be getting any kind of  welfare whether it's done through the  tax code or not the whole concept of  welfare to to libertarians is  troublesome of course it's it's  troublesome in that you are patronizing  people by taking money from some people  and giving it to others for starters and  it is certainly a patronizing when we  say you can't have this money unless you  do a B and C which is what I'm  suggesting and I do so with with  attached reluctance it's a little like a  parent saying to a child yeah you can  get your $25 allowance but only if you  pick up your room that kind of  patronizing behavior to us libertarians  is repugnant but in the situation that  we have now it is better to put  conditions on on welfare payments than  to simply go on dishing them out and  perpetuating poverty in the ways that  that we have described you know we live  in a world now where we expect our  worker most of us expect our work to be  expressive of our values and of our  lifestyle and what we care about or who  we are is that you know how do you bring  that down to a lower level of you know  service economy or jobs that generally  are not particularly you know that  glamorous it seems well I think that any  job is better than no job and most of  the people that I interviewed that said  they'd rather be working it wasn't they  didn't want to be on welfare that was  not how they envisioned their life would  be  I heard that phrase over and over again  that I thought I was going to be part of  the American Dream I was going to have a  house I was gonna have a car and two  little kids and go to work every day and  now I'm just surviving  I'm just getting by on these checks that  come in the mail and and all