 of them  said you know I would rather be the one  buying stuff for my kids I would rather  be the one paying the rent you know I  don't want to be in this situation  there's no hope here and so I think the  argument that it has to be a glamorous  job of some kind isn't necessarily  accurate I think that most people I mean  remember your first jobs my first jobs  Phil's first jobs probably weren't the  most exciting jobs in the world but they  were I look back on them as incredibly  rewarding that first check no matter  what you did to get it was more  rewarding than getting something in the  mail and and so I think that's part of  it and the other part is the more people  we can get to work then they start  working on their own careers and that's  where that that pleasure of working hard  at something and well working your way  up comes about but it doesn't come about  by sitting at home and just taking  benefits and and not working people have  an innate desire to accomplish things  everybody wants to be able to say I did  that I fed my kids I put shoes on the  baby I brought home a paycheck there are  different ways of accomplishing that in  life but for most people earning money  at a job is an important component of it  when you take that away you take away  dignity you take away a sense of  self-worth when you're accepting money  from some source that you know you could  never repay  that's bad for people they don't like it  you have an unrepairable death so that  when we talk about the issue of  happiness in the early part of the book  and the importance of work to do just  fundamental human happiness lease is  absolutely right it doesn't matter if  it's a lousy job any job is better than  no job all right well we will leave it  there  I want to thank Phil Harvey and Lisa  Conyers for talking to Reason TV they  are the authors of the new book the  human cost of welfare how the system  hurts the people it's supposed to help  for is on TV I'm Nick Gillespie
 

